      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
