      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is defined only when $xy>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
