  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14101574",
    "id": "q-132732",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132732",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only'.",
